---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Cite from "../../components/compare/Cite.astro";
import CompareTable, { type Row } from "../../components/compare/CompareTable.astro";
import Sources, { type Source } from "../../components/compare/Sources.astro";
import { HELIUM_URL } from "../../config";

// Every claim about Arc below cites one of these, by number.
const sources: Source[] = [
  { title: "The Browser Company, “A farewell to Arc.”", url: "https://arc.net/" },
  { title: "Arc on Mac release notes.", url: "https://start.arc.net/release-notes" },
  { title: "Arc Terms of Use.", url: "https://start.arc.net/terms-of-use" },
  { title: "Arc, “Paint with the internet.”", url: "https://start.arc.net/paint-the-internet" },
  { title: "Arc Privacy Policy.", url: "https://arc.net/privacy" },
];

const rows: Row[] = [
  { label: "Platforms", rac: "Mac with Apple silicon (M1 or later), macOS 13 or later", other: "Mac and Windows", cite: 1 },
  { label: "Engine", rac: "Chromium, through Helium", other: "Chromium", cite: 2 },
  { label: "Source code", rac: "Open source, GPL-3.0", other: "Closed source", cite: 3 },
  { label: "Account", rac: "None. rac has no accounts", other: "Arc account. No new accounts since October 22, 2026", cite: 1 },
  { label: "Spaces", rac: "Yes", other: "Yes", cite: 1 },
  { label: "Pinned tabs and favorites", rac: "Yes", other: "Yes", cite: 1 },
  { label: "Your Arc setup", rac: "Imports spaces, pinned tabs, favorites, and themes", other: "Save your open tabs as a list of links before December 22, 2026", cite: 1 },
  { label: "Status", rac: "Free, with updates from GitHub releases", other: "Stops working on December 22, 2026", cite: 1 },
];
---

<Base title="rac and Arc · rac" description="How rac compares with Arc: what carries over, what rac does differently, and what Arc does that rac doesn’t.">
  <article class="frame grid gap-x-10 py-[clamp(56px,9vw,128px)] lg:grid-cols-12">
    <header class="self-start lg:sticky lg:top-10 lg:col-span-5">
      <p class="text-[0.9375rem] font-medium text-(--ink-2)"><a class="text-link" href="/compare">Compare</a></p>
      <h1 class="display-xl mt-4">rac and Arc</h1>
      <p class="text-lede mt-8 max-w-[34ch]">
        rac exists because of Arc. Here’s what carries over, and what doesn’t.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose-rac mt-14 lg:col-span-6 lg:col-start-7 lg:mt-3">
      <p>
        Arc is The Browser Company’s browser for Mac and Windows. The Browser Company says Arc will stop working on
        December 22, 2026, and it points Arc users to its newer browser, Dia.<Cite n={1} /> rac is a separate, open-source
        browser for the Mac, built on <a class="text-link" href={HELIUM_URL}>Helium</a>. It keeps what many people
        miss most about Arc: spaces, pinned tabs, favorites, and a sidebar that hides. It can bring your Arc setup
        across. It doesn’t do everything Arc did.
      </p>

      <CompareTable other="Arc" rows={rows} caption="rac and Arc, side by side" />

      <h2>Where rac is different</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>rac is open source under GPL-3.0. You can read every line, and so can anyone else.</li>
        <li>
          There are no rac accounts. Arc has accounts, and its privacy policy says it may collect product usage data,
          such as how often you use various features.<Cite n={5} /> rac collects no telemetry, shows no ads,
          and nothing in it phones home to rac.
        </li>
        <li>
          Arc import keeps your spaces as spaces, with their pinned tabs, favorites, and themes. Press{" "}
          <kbd>⌘S</kbd> to hide the sidebar.
        </li>
        <!-- AI wording needs Core sign-off (G7) -->
        <li>
          AI is off by default, and with it off, no AI appears anywhere. If you turn it on, you pick the model: OpenAI,
          Anthropic, Google, OpenRouter, a local model in Ollama or LM Studio, or an agent you already have, like Claude
          Code or Codex.
        </li>
        <!-- AI wording needs Core sign-off (G7) -->
        <li>rac is Helium with rac’s changes applied as patches on top, so Helium’s privacy work comes with it.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Where Arc is better or does more</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Arc runs on Windows as well as the Mac.<Cite n={1} /> rac is Mac only, on Apple silicon.</li>
        <li>
          Boosts let you build your own changes to websites right in Arc, and Easels are canvases for saving and sharing
          what you find online.<Cite n={4} /> rac has neither, and its import doesn’t bring them.
        </li>
        <li>
          Arc has Peek for previewing links, Little Arc for quick searches, and Air Traffic Control for sending links to
          the right space.<Cite n={2} /> rac doesn’t have these.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Who should pick which</h2>
      <!-- AI wording needs Core sign-off (G7) -->
      <p>
        If you want The Browser Company’s next browser, or you need Windows, Dia is where Arc is sending its users.<Cite n={1} />{" "}
        See <a class="text-link" href="/compare/dia">rac and Dia</a>. If you want Arc’s spaces, pinned tabs, and hiding
        sidebar on your Mac, with no account and AI only if you ask for it, try rac. If you don’t need any of Arc’s
        ideas and want a plain, private Chromium browser, use{" "}
        <a class="text-link" href="/compare/helium">Helium</a> itself.
      </p>
      <!-- AI wording needs Core sign-off (G7) -->

      <Sources sources={sources} checked="2026-10-28" />
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
